## Functional Interfaces and Lambdas

```java
// Function<T, R> — takes T, returns R
Function<String, Integer> length = s -> s.length();
Function<User, String> getName = User::getName;  // Method reference

// Compose functions:
Function<String, String> trim = String::trim;
Function<String, String> upper = String::toUpperCase;
Function<String, String> trimAndUpper = trim.andThen(upper);
// trimAndUpper.apply("  hello  ") → "HELLO"

// Consumer<T> — takes T, no return
Consumer<String> print = System.out::println;
Consumer<User> sendEmail = user -> emailService.send(user.getEmail());
// Combine:
Consumer<User> logAndEmail = sendEmail.andThen(u -> log.info("Sent to {}", u.getEmail()));

// Supplier<T> — no input, returns T (lazy value)
Supplier<List<Order>> lazyOrders = () -> orderRepo.findAll();  // Not called yet
List<Order> orders = lazyOrders.get();  // Called here
// Used in: orElseGet(), computeIfAbsent(), @Lazy

// Predicate<T> — tests condition
Predicate<Order> isActive = order -> order.getStatus() == OrderStatus.ACTIVE;
Predicate<Order> isHighValue = order -> order.getTotal().compareTo(BigDecimal.valueOf(1000)) > 0;
// Compose:
Predicate<Order> isHighValueActive = isActive.and(isHighValue);
Predicate<Order> eitherCondition = isActive.or(isHighValue);
Predicate<Order> notActive = isActive.negate();

List<Order> filtered = orders.stream()
    .filter(isHighValueActive)
    .collect(toList());
```

## Custom Functional Interface

```java
@FunctionalInterface
public interface Transformer<T, R, E extends Exception> {
    R transform(T input) throws E;  // Can declare checked exception
    // @FunctionalInterface ensures exactly one abstract method
}

// Usage:
Transformer<String, User, UserNotFoundException> userLookup =
    email -> userRepo.findByEmail(email)
        .orElseThrow(() -> new UserNotFoundException(email));

User user = userLookup.transform("user@example.com");

// Compare: java.util.function.Function can't throw checked exceptions
// Custom functional interfaces solve this
```

## Method Reference Types

```java
// Type 1: Static method reference
Function<String, Integer> parser = Integer::parseInt;  // = s -> Integer.parseInt(s)
List<Integer> numbers = List.of("1","2","3").stream()
    .map(Integer::parseInt).collect(toList());

// Type 2: Instance method on specific object
String prefix = "ID-";
Function<String, String> addPrefix = prefix::concat;  // = s -> prefix.concat(s)

// Type 3: Instance method on arbitrary object
Function<String, String> toUpper = String::toUpperCase;  // = s -> s.toUpperCase()
Comparator<String> byLength = Comparator.comparingInt(String::length);
// = Comparator.comparingInt(s -> s.length())

// Type 4: Constructor reference
Function<String, User> createUser = User::new;  // = name -> new User(name)
Supplier<List<String>> listFactory = ArrayList::new;  // = () -> new ArrayList<>()
```

## Variable Capture — Effectively Final

```java
// Lambdas can capture local variables only if effectively final:
String category = "electronics";  // Effectively final (not reassigned)
Predicate<Product> filter = p -> p.getCategory().equals(category);  // OK

// ILLEGAL: reassigning captured variable
String category2 = "electronics";
category2 = "books";  // Now NOT effectively final
Predicate<Product> filter2 = p -> p.getCategory().equals(category2);  // COMPILE ERROR

// Workaround for mutable capture: use a holder or AtomicReference
AtomicReference<String> mutableCategory = new AtomicReference<>("electronics");
Predicate<Product> filter3 = p -> p.getCategory().equals(mutableCategory.get());
mutableCategory.set("books");  // Can modify the reference content
// Note: lambda sees mutableCategory.get() at execution time, not capture time
```

## Production Reality

Functional interfaces enable the strategy pattern without creating explicit classes. When Spring's `List<SomeProcessor>` injection collects all implementations, you could also wire lambdas as `@Bean` — each lambda IS a functional interface implementation. Use `BiFunction`, `BiConsumer`, and `BiPredicate` when two inputs are needed. For three or more, define a custom functional interface — keep it readable.

## Core Operations

```java
List<Order> orders = orderRepo.findAll();

// filter + map + collect:
List<String> pendingOrderIds = orders.stream()
    .filter(o -> o.getStatus() == OrderStatus.PENDING)
    .filter(o -> o.getTotal().compareTo(BigDecimal.valueOf(100)) > 0)
    .map(o -> o.getId().toString())
    .collect(Collectors.toList());

// flatMap — flatten: Order has multiple items
List<OrderItem> allItems = orders.stream()
    .flatMap(o -> o.getItems().stream())  // One order → many items
    .collect(Collectors.toList());

// groupingBy — group orders by status:
Map<OrderStatus, List<Order>> byStatus = orders.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Order::getStatus));

// groupingBy with downstream collector:
Map<OrderStatus, Long> countByStatus = orders.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Order::getStatus, Collectors.counting()));

Map<String, BigDecimal> totalByUser = orders.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
        o -> o.getUser().getName(),
        Collectors.mapping(Order::getTotal, Collectors.reducing(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add))
    ));

// toMap — list to map by ID:
Map<Long, Order> orderById = orders.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.toMap(Order::getId, Function.identity()));

// Joining — concatenate strings:
String orderIds = orders.stream()
    .map(o -> o.getId().toString())
    .collect(Collectors.joining(", ", "[", "]"));
// Output: [1, 2, 3, 4]
```

## Lazy Evaluation

```java
// Lazy: only processes elements as needed
Optional<Order> firstExpensive = orders.stream()
    .filter(o -> {  // Each element passed through as needed
        System.out.println("Filtering: " + o.getId());
        return o.getTotal().compareTo(BigDecimal.valueOf(1000)) > 0;
    })
    .findFirst();  // Stops after finding first — may not process all elements

// Short-circuit: if order[3] is the first expensive one:
// Only orders 0, 1, 2, 3 are filtered — not the rest of the list

// Build pipeline then execute:
Stream<Order> pipeline = orders.stream()
    .filter(Order::isActive)
    .sorted(Comparator.comparing(Order::getCreatedAt).reversed());
// Nothing executed yet!

long count = pipeline.count();  // Now it executes
// But: can't reuse the stream after terminal operation — streams are single-use!
```

## reduce and Advanced Operations

```java
// reduce: fold elements into a single value
BigDecimal totalRevenue = orders.stream()
    .map(Order::getTotal)
    .reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add);
// Same as: Collectors.summingDouble()

// Statistics:
IntSummaryStatistics stats = orders.stream()
    .mapToInt(o -> o.getItems().size())
    .summaryStatistics();
System.out.printf("Min: %d, Max: %d, Avg: %.2f%n",
    stats.getMin(), stats.getMax(), stats.getAverage());

// Partition by predicate:
Map<Boolean, List<Order>> partition = orders.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(
        o -> o.getTotal().compareTo(BigDecimal.valueOf(500)) > 0
    ));
List<Order> highValue = partition.get(true);
List<Order> lowValue  = partition.get(false);

// teeing collector (Java 12+) — two collectors in one pass:
record Stats(BigDecimal total, long count) {}
Stats orderStats = orders.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.teeing(
        Collectors.reducing(BigDecimal.ZERO, Order::getTotal, BigDecimal::add),
        Collectors.counting(),
        Stats::new
    ));
```

## When NOT to Use Streams

```java
// TOO COMPLEX — use loop instead:
// If you need to break out of the middle with complex state:
boolean found = false;
for (Order order : orders) {
    if (order.isValid() && processOrder(order)) {  // processOrder has side effects
        found = true;
        break;  // Clear intent
    }
}

// Avoid: stream with external state mutation:
int[] count = {0};  // Hack to mutate in lambda
orders.stream().filter(o -> {
    count[0]++;   // Side effect — not thread-safe, unclear intent
    return o.isActive();
}).collect(toList());
// Use: Collectors.counting() instead

// Simple loops are often MORE readable:
// Stream: orders.stream().filter(Order::isActive).findFirst().orElseThrow()
// Loop:   for (Order o : orders) { if (o.isActive()) return o; } throw ...
// Same performance, clearer for simple cases
```

## Production Reality

Streams shine for data transformation pipelines: convert a list of entities to DTOs, group by some criteria, aggregate totals. They're less appropriate for complex iteration with multiple exit conditions or significant side effects. A useful heuristic: if you need a `for` label or `break`, use a loop. If you're transforming data from one form to another, streams are likely cleaner.

## Correct Optional Usage

```java
// Return Optional when absence is expected and meaningful:
public Optional<User> findByEmail(String email) {
    return userRepository.findByEmail(email);
}

// Consumer of Optional — correct patterns:
Optional<User> user = findByEmail(email);

// Pattern 1: orElseThrow() — most common for 'should exist' cases
User found = findByEmail(email)
    .orElseThrow(() -> new UserNotFoundException("User not found: " + email));

// Pattern 2: orElse() — provide default
String name = findByEmail(email)
    .map(User::getName)
    .orElse("Unknown User");

// Pattern 3: orElseGet() — lazily compute default (expensive fallback)
User user2 = findByEmail(email)
    .orElseGet(() -> createGuestUser(email));  // Only called if empty

// Pattern 4: ifPresent() — run code if value exists
findByEmail(email).ifPresent(u -> emailService.sendWelcome(u));

// Pattern 5: ifPresentOrElse() (Java 9+)
findByEmail(email).ifPresentOrElse(
    u -> log.info("Found user: {}", u.getName()),
    () -> log.warn("User not found for: {}", email)
);

// Pattern 6: filter + map chain
Optional<String> adminEmail = findByEmail(email)
    .filter(User::isAdmin)   // Empty if user is not admin
    .map(User::getEmail)     // Transform if still present
    .filter(e -> e.endsWith(".com"));  // Another filter

// Pattern 7: flatMap — when map returns Optional
Optional<Address> address = findByEmail(email)
    .flatMap(User::getPrimaryAddress);  // User::getPrimaryAddress returns Optional<Address>
// flatMap prevents Optional<Optional<Address>>
```

## Anti-Patterns

```java
// ANTI-PATTERN 1: isPresent() + get() — worse than null check
Optional<User> opt = findByEmail(email);
if (opt.isPresent()) {
    User u = opt.get();  // Verbose — use orElse/orElseThrow
    processUser(u);
}
// Better: findByEmail(email).ifPresent(this::processUser)

// ANTI-PATTERN 2: Optional as field
public class User {
    private Optional<String> phoneNumber;  // DON'T — Serialization issues, unnecessary
    // Use: private String phoneNumber;  // null = not provided
}

// ANTI-PATTERN 3: Optional as parameter
public void createUser(String name, Optional<String> phone) {  // DON'T
    // Use method overloading or nullable parameter instead
}

// ANTI-PATTERN 4: Returning null Optional
public Optional<User> findById(Long id) {
    if (id == null) return null;  // NEVER return null from Optional-returning method!
    // Return: Optional.empty()
}

// ANTI-PATTERN 5: Using Optional.get() without check
Optional<User> opt = findByEmail(email);
User u = opt.get();  // Throws NoSuchElementException if empty — NO BENEFIT OVER NULL
```

## Optional in Stream Context

```java
// Java 9+: Optional.stream() — integrate with Stream API
List<String> names = emails.stream()
    .map(userService::findByEmail)  // Stream<Optional<User>>
    .filter(Optional::isPresent)    // Remove empties
    .map(Optional::get)             // Unwrap
    .map(User::getName)
    .collect(toList());

// Simpler with Java 9 flatMap:
List<String> names = emails.stream()
    .flatMap(email -> userService.findByEmail(email).stream())  // Optional.stream()
    .map(User::getName)
    .collect(toList());
```

## Production Reality

Spring Data `findById()` returns `Optional<T>` — get comfortable with `.orElseThrow()` for the common case where the entity must exist. For service methods returning search results, returning `Optional` is appropriate. For internal helper methods where null would never occur, return the value directly — over-using Optional adds cognitive overhead without value.
